---
import LetsTalk from '@components/home/LetsTalk.astro'
import Insight from '@components/insights/Insight.astro'
import InsightSignoff from '@components/insights/InsightSignoff.astro'
import Layout from '@layouts/Layout.astro'
import { getInsightNav } from '@data/insights'
import InsightNav from '@components/insights/InsightNav.astro'

const { previous, current, next } = getInsightNav('what-is-a-headless-cms-and-do-i-actually-need-one')
---

<Layout title={current?.title} description={current?.summary}>
  <Insight tags={current?.tags}>
    <span slot="insight-title">{current?.title}</span>
    <span slot="insight-subtitle">{current?.dateLabel}</span>
    <span slot="insight-text">
      <p>
        A headless CMS is one of those terms agencies throw around without much explanation. Here is what it
        actually means, and the honest answer on whether you need one.
      </p><br />
      <p>
        A normal CMS, a standard WordPress site is the classic example, bundles two things together: where your
        content lives, and how it gets displayed. A headless CMS splits those apart. The CMS only stores and serves
        content through an API, "headless" because it has no front-end attached, and a separate system decides how
        that content actually gets shown, a website, an app, or something else entirely.
      </p><br />
      <p><strong>Worth it when:</strong></p><br />
      <ul class="lg:mt-4 flex flex-col gap-4">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>The same content needs to show up in more than one place:</strong> a website, a mobile app, maybe
            digital signage, all pulling from a single source instead of duplicating content management across
            separate systems.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>Your content structure is genuinely complex:</strong> products, locations, articles, each needing
            different structured fields that a normal page-and-post CMS starts to strain against.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>Front-end performance matters a lot:</strong> headless setups are usually built as fast, modern
            front-ends rather than constrained by a CMS's own templating system.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <br />
      <p><strong>Probably not worth it when:</strong></p><br />
      <ul class="lg:mt-4 flex flex-col gap-4">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>You only have one website and no plans for another channel:</strong> this is usually solving a
            problem you do not have.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>You want your team to update content without a developer:</strong> editors often lose the simple
            "edit it and see exactly how it looks" experience, since content is decoupled from how it is displayed,
            unless extra tooling gets built specifically for that.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>Budget and timeline are tight:</strong> you are building the entire front-end from scratch instead
            of using an existing theme, which costs more and takes longer than a standard CMS build.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>Structural changes need to happen without a developer involved:</strong> with a headless setup,
            that usually is not possible. With a normal CMS, someone in-house can often rearrange things themselves
            with a page builder.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <br />
      <p>
        For most small and medium businesses running a single website, a standard CMS is still the right call.
        Headless earns its complexity once you actually have multiple channels to feed, or content structured enough
        to need it. If you are not sure which camp you fall into, that is exactly the kind of thing worth talking
        through in a
        <a href="/services/discovery" class="underline underline-offset-2">discovery phase</a> before you commit to
        either approach.
      </p><br />
      <InsightSignoff />
    </span>
  </Insight>

  <InsightNav previousInsight={previous} nextInsight={next} />

  <LetsTalk config={{ buttons: 'both', primaryCta: 'message' }} />
</Layout>
